In this new episode of our podcast series Business Regards Croisés, we interviewed Thomas Vidal, General Manager of the Andaz Tokyo Toranomon Hills, one of Tokyo’s most iconic luxury lifestyle hotels. The hotel has established itself as a unique hybrid destination, serving not only as a place to stay but also as a hub for culture and events, where the overall experience is as important as the accommodation itself.
A graduate of ESSEC Business School, Thomas Vidal has built most of his career in Asia, particularly in Japan, where he has lived for more than twelve years.&nbsp;
“At Tokyo, luxury hospitality has become a playground for experiences”&nbsp;
Luxury hospitality as a complete experience&nbsp;
Tokyo is now establishing itself as a major destination in the global luxury market, driven by the rebound of international tourism after Covid. Long considered less central than Paris, London, or New York in this segment, the city is now experiencing rapid growth. The market nevertheless remains deeply shaped by a strong local identity, structured around omotenashi and historic hotels with highly codified standards. This specificity forces international hotel groups to adapt their offerings to particularly demanding expectations. After the pandemic, the sector rebounded quickly, notably through the rise of domestic tourism and the staycation phenomenon. In this context, hotels are no longer limited to accommodation alone.&nbsp;
Japan: a meticulous and evolving clientele&nbsp;
While Japanese guests continue to be defined by extremely high standards and attention to detail, younger generations are rapidly shifting toward more experiential expectations, seeking immersive, modern, and visually appealing stays.&nbsp;
Within this culture of extreme attention to detail, certain everyday aspects of hotel life take on particular importance. Slippers are a symbolic example: from the moment guests enter their room, their presence, quality, and comfort directly contribute to the perception of service. This level of precision, sometimes barely noticeable to international guests, can have a real impact on overall satisfaction.&nbsp;
“If the slippers are hard to find, and especially if they are not of very good quality, you lose quite a few points in guest satisfaction.” &nbsp;
Management and corporate culture in an international environment&nbsp;
In such a complex environment, Thomas Vidal insists on one central principle: people make the difference. Management is built around a leadership philosophy focused on taking care of employees. In highly international hotels such as those in Tokyo, managing multicultural teams becomes a daily challenge, yet this diversity fuels creativity, encourages innovation, and strengthens the ability of hotels to cater to an increasingly global clientele.&nbsp;
Thomas Vidal’s career reflects the current transformation of luxury hospitality in Japan: a sector undergoing hybridization, where traditional Japanese excellence meets international experience-driven standards. Between the tourism boom, the rise of lifestyle hospitality, and the reinvention of hospitality formats, Tokyo is more than ever establishing itself as a major experimental ground for the world’s leading luxury hotel players.&nbsp;

Andaz Tokyo Toranomon Hills

In this new episode of Business Cross Perspectives, we meet Malik Roumane, President &amp; CEO of JCDecaux Japan (MCDecaux). Drawing on a rich career spanning industry, luxury, and B2B, he shares his vision of a unique model that combines innovation, public service, and local adaptation in Japan’s demanding market.&nbsp;
"Everyone has to benefit from it: the city, the user, and the advertiser."&nbsp;
Malik Roumane’s career path&nbsp;
Malik Roumane’s journey in Japan follows a three-stage trajectory. He began in industry, notably with Saint-Gobain, working between France and Japan, where he developed strong expertise in structured and technical environments. He then spent around ten years in the luxury sector with Richemont before transitioning into B2B.&nbsp;
This third chapter led him to manage various business activities in Japan, culminating in his appointment as head of JCDecaux three and a half years ago. This diversity of experience is now a key asset in the way he leads the company, combining operational excellence, brand sensitivity, and a deep understanding of local dynamics.&nbsp;
JCDecaux: a unique model serving cities&nbsp;
Founded in 1964, JCDecaux is built on a simple yet revolutionary idea: financing street furniture through advertising. This model enables cities to benefit from modern, well-maintained, and evolving infrastructure without direct cost to public finances.&nbsp;
Beyond bus shelters, the offering has significantly expanded over time to include digital services, connectivity, charging solutions, and even initiatives contributing to urban greening. The challenge is no longer just to install infrastructure, but to meet the evolving needs of cities and their residents with a long-term perspective.&nbsp;
A pioneering presence in Japan&nbsp;
JCDecaux’s entry into Japan is a textbook case. At the time, advertising in public spaces was not permitted, making the model initially inapplicable. It required extensive engagement with authorities, particularly the MLIT, to demonstrate its benefits.&nbsp;
The first implementation took place in Yokohama, followed by gradual expansion to other major cities, including Tokyo. A key factor in this success was the creation of a joint venture with Mitsubishi Corporation, leading to the birth of MCDecaux. This partnership helped legitimize the model and facilitate its adoption within a specific cultural and regulatory context.&nbsp;
In a Japanese urban environment often perceived as visually saturated, JCDecaux has chosen to prioritize quality over quantity. The company pays close attention to placement, integration, and surroundings to avoid visual pollution.&nbsp;
This approach helps reposition outdoor advertising as a premium medium, capable of enhancing brands rather than blending into background noise. Major advertisers, particularly in the luxury and automotive sectors, find in it a platform aligned with their image and standards.&nbsp;
Innovation and Digital&nbsp;
Technological advancements are opening up new opportunities for the industry. The growth of digital media enables far more agile and targeted delivery of advertising campaigns. Through programmatic buying, advertisers can tailor their messages based on time, location, or external factors such as weather conditions.&nbsp;
This flexibility creates new ways to engage audiences, while maintaining strong complementarity with traditional formats, which ensure continuous presence and high visibility.&nbsp;
In Japan, street furniture also plays a vital role in public communication, particularly in the event of natural disasters. Digital displays can be immediately activated by authorities to broadcast alerts, guide the public, and support crisis management. This function further strengthens JCDecaux’s position as an integrated urban player, beyond its advertising role.&nbsp;
Supporting Japan’s Internationalization&nbsp;
In a context of demographic decline, Japan is increasingly required to evolve by integrating more international talent. For Malik Roumane, this transformation is both inevitable and an opportunity.&nbsp;
The goal is to build an environment capable of balancing Japan’s unique characteristics with growing global openness. In the long term, business success will depend on the ability to create hybrid organizations that combine cultures and expertise, while maintaining the right balance within the local context.&nbsp;
