In this new episode of our Business: Crossed Perspectives series, we welcome Ana Escobedo, founder and director of&nbsp;Les Langues du Monde, a pioneering company in intercultural training and global leadership development in Japan.
« Being a good leader in Japan isn’t about being more friendly; it’s about knowing how to inspire respect within a hierarchical framework. »
Navigating the Codes of Japanese Management
When a foreign executive arrives in Japan, they quickly discover that the usual management benchmarks do not apply in the same way. The country values hierarchy, restraint, and often in contrast with the more direct Western approaches. The teams at&nbsp;Les Langues du Monde&nbsp;support these leaders by helping them establish their legitimacy in an environment where politeness and discretion prevail over confrontation.&nbsp;
Understanding a Unique Decision-Making Process
The corporate decision-making process particularly illustrates this cultural gap. Whereas Western organizations favor speed and debate, Japanese companies rely on thorough preparation and upstream consultation. An approach sometimes perceived as slow, but one that ensures smooth and harmonious implementation once the decision has been made.&nbsp;
Comprehensive and Tailored Support
The support offered by&nbsp;Les Langues du Monde&nbsp;goes far beyond language training. Each program is custom-designed, blending language learning, leadership development, and a deep understanding of Japan’s implicit cultural codes. Executives receive personalized follow-ups, practical exercises, and simulations inspired by real-life situations to learn to communicate with clarity, confidence, and intercultural sensitivity.&nbsp;
Training Leaders Who Can Bridge Cultures
By sharing its expertise,&nbsp;Les Langues du Monde&nbsp;helps foster a new generation of leaders capable of navigating across cultures, building trust, and strengthening international cooperation, an invaluable skill in a world where intercultural understanding has become a true driver of success.

On 17 March, specialist professional recruitment firm Robert Walters Japan announced the results of a survey regarding childcare leave conducted among female company employees working in Japanese and foreign-affiliated companies in Japan.&nbsp;
Childcare leave usage among female employees: 72% in foreign companies, 66% in Japanese companies
Among female professionals with children that we surveyed, 72% of those working for foreign-affiliated companies and 66% of those working for Japanese companies said they have continued to work after pregnancy and have taken childcare leave. Conversely, when female employees who did not take childcare leave were asked why they did not, the most common response was “I resigned from my job” (41%), followed by “I did not qualify since I recently changed jobs” (36%). The results indicate that many women have resigned for various reasons due to pregnancy or childbirth.&nbsp;
Returning from maternity leave: 15% experienced change in responsibilities, 40% “satisfied”&nbsp;
Regarding changes in responsibilities and workload after returning from maternity leave, 60% reported experiencing no changes while 21% said they changed jobs. Fifteen percent of female employees said their responsibilities and workload changed unintentionally after returning to work. In terms of satisfaction with job duties and workload after returning from maternity leave, 41% answered that their work was “flexible and well-balanced” or “rewarding and generally satisfying.” Thirty-one percent responded that they found their work rewarding but the heavy workload made it difficult to balance work and family, while 13% said they were able to use their time flexibly but found their work not very rewarding.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Balancing work &amp; childcare: female employees generally satisfied with support system
When asked about the level of satisfaction with their employer’s support system for balancing work and childcare, around 70% of those who took childcare leave answered that they were “satisfied” or “somewhat satisfied.” On the other hand, only 50% of female employees who did not take childcare leave but are currently working answered that they were “satisfied” or “somewhat satisfied.” This suggests that, among female employees, whether maternity leave is offered by an employer impacts satisfaction of the support system.&nbsp;
Based on the survey findings, there is room for improvement for companies to create a workplace environment where all employees can play an active role and have equal opportunities for success even while raising children.&nbsp;
more information
 (Survey period: 21-27 January &amp;17-24 February 2022, Target: Female company employees registered with Robert Walters Japan and living in Japan n=855)

